{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact head bc46912922ab6eedc6476f7b96a523d829b441fa on task/ui-personal-work-state. I found no defect in the reviewed behaviour. Every stated condition holds under experiment, not only under reading. What I do raise are nine places where the suite cannot fail if that behaviour is later reverted; each is named below with the reversion I actually applied and the regression it would let ship green.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED AND HOW\n\nHead and hygiene. The reviewed checkout /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/ui-personal-work-state is clean at exactly bc46912922ab6eedc6476f7b96a523d829b441fa. One commit sits on the branch, carrying Rests-On: \u003ce3d2519fe3aa540bc987acf3de58d58b3323a77b\u003e, the task request. git diff --check is clean. No .go file and no ThreadPane file changed; the whole diff is ui/src/lib/memory.ts, ui/src/lib/work.ts, ui/src/components/WorkDrawer.tsx, the two test files, and the embedded bundle.\n\nBundle provenance. In a fresh detached worktree at this head I ran npm ci then npm run build. git status --porcelain came back empty. The committed spike/internal/service/uidist bundle (assets/index-ilQlLIVw.js) is the output of the sources at this head, not a stale artefact.\n\nGates, in a pristine scratch worktree, all passing: make vet; make test (every package ok); go test -race ./... (every package ok); npm test 38/38. The worktree stayed clean afterwards.\n\nMutation experiments. I applied 26 single behaviour reversions one at a time in a separate scratch worktree at this head and ran npm test after each. 17 were caught, 9 were not. I never touched the reviewed worktree.\n\nRendered-component checks against the live projection. Unit tests on a pure helper can pass while the component still misbehaves, so I server-rendered the real WorkView against curl http://127.0.0.1:7777/v0/status with the real actor roster:\n\n- \"Needs my action\" rendered 69 for claude. I derived the same number independently straight from projection.commitments in Python, without going through the helper: 69, and the two lists agreed item for item, including this very review request.\n- The count is genuinely per-actor: claude 69, codex 57, hugh 3, and an unconfigured actor name renders no personal filters and no follow controls at all.\n- The decisive one. I re-rendered with every topic marked viewed at watermark 1e9. \"Needs my action\" stayed at 69; \"Unread\" fell to 0. Personal action demonstrably cannot be cleared by viewing, at the component boundary and not merely in the helper signature.\n- Per-topic watermarks. With three topics unread, setting the watermark on one dropped unread to two and cleared only that topic. Opening one topic advances only that topic.\n- Following begins at now. Following a topic authored by someone else, from its current head, left it not unread; the same topic followed from watermark -1 was unread. History does not become unread because you started following.\n- Own activity never counts. Across the live room, 14 topics offered a change and none was attributed to me. On synthetic input, a topic I author whose only post-watermark act is mine yields no change, while a later act by someone else yields theirs; when my act is the newest, the older unread act by someone else still surfaces.\n- Actor, time and status render in both presentations. List shows \"changed - codex \u003ctime\u003e - in progress\" with aria-label \"Changed since viewed by codex, status ready\". I re-rendered the board through the real WorkView (by seeding the presentation state in the scratch copy only) and it produced the same change lines plus the follow toggle with aria-pressed, across lanes available/inProgress/review and the attention section.\n- Privacy. Neither memory.ts, work.ts nor WorkDrawer.tsx contains any fetch, api call, or beacon. The keys are workroom.personal-work.\u003cgenesis\u003e.\u003cfingerprint\u003e; reverting either the genesis or the fingerprint component fails the suite. An ambiguous actor name resolves to undefined and the whole personal surface disappears; that fail-closed path is pinned by the SSR test.\n\nFINDINGS - TEST COVERAGE THAT CANNOT FAIL\n\nNone of these is a defect in the behaviour at this head. Each is a reversion I applied that the suite accepted, so the named regression would ship green.\n\n1. ui/src/lib/work.ts, filterPersonalWorkProjection. Adding \"if ((viewed[topic.event] ?? -1) \u003e= 0) return []\" to the needs branch makes \"Needs my action\" clear as soon as a topic is opened. Nothing failed. This is the condition the request puts first, and the only thing standing between it and a silent regression is that workItemNeedsAction takes no memory argument - a caller can still filter on memory, as my patch did.\n\n2. ui/src/lib/work.ts, topicChangeSince. Dropping \"item.actor !== me\" makes your own acts mark your own topics unread. Nothing failed. The existing assertions still pass because the fixture's newest act is by someone else either way.\n\n3. ui/src/components/WorkDrawer.tsx, the changes memo. Replacing the per-topic watermark with Math.max over all watermarks makes opening any one topic clear unread everywhere. Nothing failed.\n\n4. ui/src/lib/memory.ts, viewWorkTopic. Making it raise every stored watermark rather than one. Nothing failed.\n\n5. ui/src/components/WorkDrawer.tsx, BoardCard. Deleting the \u003cTopicChange\u003e line removes actor, time and status from the board entirely. Nothing failed - the SSR test only ever renders the list, because presentation state defaults to \"list\" and SSR cannot click.\n\n6. ui/src/components/WorkDrawer.tsx, BoardCard. Deleting \u003cTopicFollowButton\u003e removes follow management from the board. Nothing failed, for the same reason.\n\n7. ui/src/components/WorkDrawer.tsx, TopicFollowButton. Removing aria-pressed leaves the control with no announced on/off state. The aria-label removal is caught; the pressed state is not.\n\n8. ui/src/lib/memory.ts, loadPersonalWorkMemory. Replacing the type filtering with a bare cast means hostile or corrupt localStorage flows straight into the follow set and watermark map. Nothing failed; no test feeds it malformed JSON.\n\n9. ui/src/components/WorkDrawer.tsx, the visible memo. Passing lifecycleVisible instead of work into the personal views re-imposes the lifecycle filter, so a closed or filtered-out topic silently vanishes from \"Needs my action\". Nothing failed. The deliberate choice recorded in the comment is unpinned.\n\nItems 5 and 6 are the cheapest to close: the SSR test needs one board render, which is why I could only reach that path by seeding component state in a scratch copy.\n\nOBSERVATIONS I DELIBERATELY DID NOT RAISE AS FINDINGS\n\n- main is eight commits ahead and is not an ancestor of this head. Those commits touch only the kernel, docs and the spike README - nothing under ui - so they cannot invalidate what I checked. Rebasing is a merge-time decision, not a defect in the head.\n- The projection marks this artifact succession_unrecorded with one live predecessor at path ui: f06285a018bc5806a8fe8732d9c91fd240807e1c at 019f08ba, which is not an ancestor of this head. AGENTS.md step 5 places that supersession at merge, so it is not owed at step 3. Whoever merges should note that two live ui artifacts currently name divergent unmerged heads, and both regenerate the same bundle file.\n- The board renders one topic-scoped follow toggle per work-item card. In the live room that is 113 identical \"unfollow topic\" controls in a single view. Correct, but noisy for anyone navigating by control.\n- The three personal badges mix units: \"Needs my action\" counts work items, \"Unread\" and \"Following\" count topics.\n- The Unread badge counts topics across the whole projection while the list may hide some behind the current lifecycle filter - I saw a badge of 3 with 2 change lines visible. This is coherent, because selecting Unread bypasses the lifecycle filter and shows all 3.\n- savePersonalWorkMemory is called inside a setPersonalState updater. Side effects in a state updater are a React anti-pattern, but the write is idempotent and sequential updaters compose correctly, so no state is lost.\n- buildWorkProjection falls back to indexing statements+acts when projection.decisions is empty. That is a different ordering space from the decisions path, so watermarks would not be comparable across the two. /v0/status is documented as the complete projection and always carries decisions (1191 of 1191 at review time), so this is unreachable today; only /v0/status-summary is capped.\n- aria-label on the \u003cp\u003e inside TopicChange is unusual, but that node sits inside the topic button, so it contributes to the button's accessible name rather than being dropped.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT VERIFY\n\n- No live browser click-through. The browser-control connection is unavailable in this context, exactly as codex reported. Everything above the interaction layer I checked by server-side rendering; actual pointer and keyboard interaction, focus order, and real localStorage round-tripping in a browser remain unexercised by anyone.\n- I did not repeat codex's claim of two byte-identical consecutive builds. I verified the stronger and simpler thing once: a clean-room npm ci plus npm run build at this head reproduces the committed bundle exactly.\n- I did not audit the unchanged Work list/board projection beyond confirming those files did not change and the suite still passes.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:70e448f12caa2f623171e2dcd299f6d995cd48a7","head":"bc46912922ab6eedc6476f7b96a523d829b441fa","verdict":"approved"}}